//BUG="a break out of `while true` leaves a path that falls off the end"
//ERR any

// The loop-exemption case of 27_the_only_return_is_inside_a_while.cpl. A
// `while true` is the one loop a returns-on-all-paths check may treat as never
// falling through — see 05_functions/29_while_true_returns_from_inside.cpl —
// but that only holds while nothing can leave it. The `brk` here is a second
// exit, so the end of the body is reachable after all and `f(1000)` runs off it.
//
// The exemption therefore cannot be "the condition is the literal `true`"; it
// has to be "the condition is the literal `true` and no reachable `brk` targets
// this loop". 05_functions/31_a_while_true_with_a_break_returns_after_the_loop.cpl
// is the accepted form — the same function with a `ret -1` at the end.
//
// No //MSG, for the same reason as 25-32: the diagnostic does not exist yet.
//
// CAVEAT while `brk` is unimplemented: this case passes today for the wrong
// reason — the parser rejects `brk` itself, which is still a compile error and
// so still satisfies `//ERR any`. The harness will say the bug looks fixed. It
// is not. Keep the //BUG line until `brk` compiles and this case goes red on
// the missing-return check.

fn f n
    i = 0
    while true
        if i > 10
            brk
        if i * i > n
            ret i
        i++

fn main
    out "{f(1000)}"
